--- body ---
## Context

Observed three times in the 2026-11-09 TLC sprint (MOE session `CNRS` on
padme), in three unrelated project repos, each by a different team lead:

- sen-causal-reading PR #169: the first gaze answer was ESCALATE because the
  project's `make check` left build output in the reviewer's worktree; a
  second run answered REVIEWED and the gate accepted that one. The stale
  ESCALATE comment is still visible on the PR.
- chemin-de-voix (PRs #182/#183): a full `make test` writes two git-ignored
  build files (`output/05_manuscript/.assemble.stamp`, `main.docx`); gaze's
  cleanup counted them as leftovers and turned its result into ESCALATE. The
  same lead also saw one gaze run delete the tracked
  `output/05_manuscript/*.md` files in its own review copy, then restore
  them; nothing outside the copy was affected.
- fuzzy-corpus PR #72: the gate's removal of its temporary worktree should
  have stopped on leftover LaTeX build files and went ahead anyway (the
  mirror-image failure: the check did not fire when it should have); only
  regenerable files were lost.

Each false ESCALATE costs a second gaze run and leaves a misleading comment
on the PR. The defect is in the harness, not the projects: the files are
git-ignored build products the project's own check target creates.

## Observation, cause not yet established

`gaze` (and the gate's worktree removal) treat any untracked file in the
review copy as a leftover, without consulting `.gitignore`. Candidate fix:
count only files that `git status --porcelain --ignored=no` reports (i.e.
untracked AND not ignored) as leftovers; ignored build products are the
project's business and `git worktree remove --force` on them is safe.
Reproduce first with a positive control: a review copy holding one ignored
file and one untracked-unignored file.

## Exit criteria

- [x] A reproduction shows the false ESCALATE on an ignored file and the correct ESCALATE on an untracked, unignored file
- [x] gaze and the gate's cleanup agree on the same leftover rule
- [x] The second observation (tracked files deleted then restored inside the review copy) is either reproduced and ticketed separately or recorded as not reproducible

## Imagine + Plan (raid a8b3e4, 2026-11-09)

Premise refined: the one decision site is `worktree_residue` in
`scripts/worktree-clean-lib.sh`, which counts ignored files on purpose
(ticket 1212: a `*` ignore hides drafts; pinned in
`tests/test_worktree_tools.py`). gaze (`scripts/gaze_close.py`) and the gate
(`skills/verify-gate/SKILL.md`) both call `worktree-exit-preflight.sh
--removal` then plain `git worktree remove`, so they already share one rule;
the fix must keep them aligned. The ticket's candidate fix (ignored=no
everywhere) would reopen 1212 for roar, lair and gc.

Actions: an opt-in `--ignored-ok` flag on `worktree-exit-preflight.sh`
dropping `!!` records, refused unless the tree's basename is `review-*` or
`gate-*` (throwaway checkouts of a pushed head); gaze_close and the gate's
removal pass it after `--removal`; one line in
`skills/gaze/references/cleanup.md`. Tests in a new test file (the peer raid's
open PR #2348 owns `tests/test_worktree_tools.py`): ignored file with flag ->
0, untracked unignored -> 1 naming it, ignored without flag -> 1, flag on a
non-review tree -> refused; gaze_close cleanup on each case.

Criterion 3: no harness path runs checkout/restore/clean in a review copy;
likely the project's make target regenerating `output/05_manuscript/*.md`.
Record as not reproducible. The fuzzy-corpus mirror case is not reproducible
from current code either.

Out of scope, reported: `worktree-gc.sh` keeps the strict rule for stranded
review/gate trees; its files belong to PR #2348.
